      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Phantom urinary phenomena are a relatively rare disease entity. Literature search has revealed only one case following cystectomy, seven cases following spinal cord injury and several other cases in hemodialysis patients. This report presents a case of painful phantom bladder following cystectomy for chronic kidney and urinary tract infection. Treatment was directed toward sensory hyperstimulation for suppression of the subjective experience of the painful phantom. Lumbar sympathetic blocks and transcutaneous electrical stimulation were used. Competent coping mechanisms were increased through relaxation training and assertiveness training to deal with a medical problem which has no standard solution. The patient's response to the Comprehensive Pain Control Program was excellent with an estimated 75% reduction in painful phantom perceptions. A brief discussion of the medical literature on the subject is presented."},
